# EcoSuit MK.I — Exoskeleton Actuator Types
**Internal frame · ceramic-hemp plate armor · Iron Man packaging constraints**

The EcoSuit frame is not a warehouse exo and not a movie suit. The actuator only has to beat three loads: the suit’s own joint stiffness, the crewmember’s work (tools, habitat, fall recovery), and whatever gravity you are standing in. Everything else is packaging, heat, vacuum, and what happens when it dies mid-EVA.

---

## 1. Load budget

Pressurized-suit data is the right starting budget. Empty-suit elbow torques are only a few newton-metres; with a human inside they climb.

| Joint | Order-of-magnitude budget | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Elbow | ~15–18 N·m class | High cycle, tool work |
| Knee | ~30–40 N·m class | Walk, kneel, stairs |
| Hip flexion | ~40–70 N·m class | Assist may be ~50% of peak |
| Hip abduction | can exceed 100 N·m | Do not power this first |
| Glove | force-assist / fatigue | Different problem than gait |

Reference points:
- Advanced-suit / S3-class requirement bands: elbow ~15–18 N·m, knee ~32–40 N·m, hip flex ~41–72 N·m, hip abd much higher.
- EVA-assist patent example: hip −50 to 140 N·m, knee −30 to 40 N·m, targeting ~50% assist.
- NASA X1: series-elastic motors at hips and knees for this class of problem.
- Gloves are a separate war: pressurized-glove fatigue, not walking torque.

That budget already kills a lot of lab actuators.

---

## 2. Design rules (before picking hardware)

1. Power only the sagittal work joints in MK.I.
2. Default-on-failure = free or clutched-open, never locked.
3. Actuator LRU removes without pulling ceramic plates.
4. Impedance control. The human generates the path. The exo cancels suit hysteresis and adds a boost cap.
5. Heat and vacuum beat peak-torque on the spec sheet.
6. Distal mass is metabolic cost and plate-clash risk. Get motors off the limb when you can.
7. Do not use suit oxygen as a pneumatic working fluid.

Movie suits pretend every joint is a sealed rotary servo with infinite power and no heat. Real Iron-Man-adjacent hardware (Sarcos, NASA X1 / IHMC) motorized hip/knee (and later ankle) and left sidestep, turn, and toe-point passive.

EcoSuit plates add one more rule: if a motor dies, you open a dorsal hatch or pop a segment. You do not depot-strip a pauldron on the lunar surface.

