## 3. Actuator families

### 3.1 Rigid electric — motor at the joint
Brushless DC (or PMSM) + gearbox (harmonic, planetary, cycloidal) sitting on the hinge.

**Why people use it:** mature, easy to control, no fluid, high bandwidth, simple power architecture (batteries + bus). Most published exoskeletons use electric power; brushed/brushless motors dominate.

**EcoSuit fit:** good for knee and elbow if the can is small enough to hide under a plate segment. Bad for shoulder and hip if you park a harmonic drive in the joint — those cans fight the Iron Man silhouette and dump heat into the hemp core.

**Must-haves:**
- High-ratio gearboxes are not backdrivable. A seized harmonic drive becomes a locked suit joint. That is a crew-safety item.
- Add a clutch, a series spring, or a passive freewheel path.
- Vacuum-rated grease or dry lube. Harmonic drives hate the wrong lubricant in vacuum.

### 3.2 Series elastic / quasi-direct-drive (SEA, QDD)
Motor + reduction + a spring you can measure. Torque ≈ spring deflection. NASA X1 and the IHMC lineage used rotary SEAs at hip and knee for this reason.

**Why it belongs on EcoSuit:**
- Command impedance, not just position. Soft when the crew moves, stiff when they plant a tool.
- Shock from a stumble or plate impact goes into the spring, not the gearbox teeth.
- Force control is good enough to cancel suit hysteresis instead of fighting the human.

**Cost:** the spring eats volume. Under overlapping ceramic segments that is the real constraint, not the motor physics.

Quasi-direct-drive (large-gap torque motor, ~6:1–10:1, no harmonic) is the current Earth-exo fashion. Better backdrive, worse mass. On the Moon that mass penalty shrinks; in the launch stack it does not.

### 3.3 Cable / Bowden / tendon drive
Motors live on the dorsal lattice or PLSS. Steel or synthetic lines run to the joint.

This is how RoboGlove / Robonaut-derived assist gloves pulled ~220 N peak grasp with forearm linear actuators. It is also how many soft upper-limb exos keep distal mass down.

**EcoSuit fit: default for anything distal.**
- Elbow, wrist, glove, maybe ankle.
- Motors sit in the cooled, serviceable volume behind the hatch — next to the PLSS, not inside a pauldron.
- Cables thread through the exo longerons. Plates never see a motor can.

**Taxes:**
- Bowden friction is a control problem and a heat problem. It changes with bend radius, temperature, and dust.
- Cable stretch looks like compliance until it looks like slop.
- A cut cable is a single-point failure unless you run antagonists in pairs.
- Lunar / Martian dust in a sheath will destroy a tendon system if you let it.

Add a series spring at the distal end and you get a cable-SEA — often the right compromise.

### 3.4 Hydraulics and electrohydrostatic (EHA)
Classic hydraulics: pump + valves + hoses + cylinders. Highest force density. Early Sarcos “Iron Man” work was hydraulic and burned kilowatts; later efficiency work got walking-with-load down toward hundreds of watts. Lesson: hydraulics are only justified if you need the force and you win the power war.

**Inside a pressure garment this is almost always the wrong answer.** A leak is a contamination event, a slip hazard, and a fire/toxicity review. Vacuum-side hydraulics need a closed loop that never sees the cabin or the suit volume.

**Electrohydrostatic actuator (EHA)** — a tiny pump married to a cylinder as one LRU, no ship-wide plumbing — is the only hydraulic variant worth keeping on the table. High force at hip/knee for 1g or loaded construction. Still a fluid. Still a seal. Still a thermal lump. Use only if electric + cable cannot make the torque at the mass you will accept.

### 3.5 Pneumatics and pneumatic artificial muscles (PAM / McKibben / bubble muscles)
Air muscles contract when inflated. Intrinsic compliance, high force-to-weight in the muscle itself, ugly when you count the compressor and tank. Used in EVA-glove exo concepts because they are light at the finger. Soft bubble muscles have also been proposed for hypogravity loading suits.

**Space problem:** you do not vent air overboard for eight hours of EVA. A closed pneumatic loop is a compressor, dryer, and accumulator on the PLSS. Compressors hate vacuum heat rejection. PAM hysteresis is real. Bandwidth is lower than a good electric joint.

**Where it still wins:** glove and maybe elbow, if the gas is a closed loop shared with an existing pressure system and you accept “assist, not position servo.”

### 3.6 Passive and semi-passive — springs, clutches, brakes
No motor. A spring dumps energy in at the right phase of gait. A clutch decides when the spring is in the loop.

**This should be the first layer of EcoSuit, not the last.**
- Suit joints already have hysteresis. A well-placed spring can cancel the rising part of that curve so the crew is not doing isometric work all day.
- Gravity-balance at the shoulder (even in 1/6 g) is cheap mass that never needs a battery.
- A clutch that opens on power loss is your locked-joint escape path.

X1 mixed four powered DOF with six passive DOF for exactly this reason — do not motorize sidestep and toe-point if a bearing and a spring will do.

### 3.7 Smart materials — SMA, EAP / DEA, vacuum-gap electrostatic
**SMA wire:** tiny, huge force-to-weight, slow, thermally inefficient, cycle-life limited. Already built into a soft EVA-glove exo (Flexinol-class wire, ~35 N per 0.5 mm wire). Fine for fingers. Not fine for a knee. Heat is the enemy next to a hemp core.

**Dielectric elastomers:** high voltage, historically unreliable, materials not automatically space-clean.

**Vacuum-gap electrostatic multilayer actuators (2025):** they use vacuum as the dielectric. Demonstrated ~0.7 g devices, >4 N, >100 Hz, peak ~1.4 kW/kg, space-polymer stack. That is gripper / small-joint / plate-deployer territory, not hip-walk territory — yet. Worth a coupon program for visor mechanisms, plate latches, and maybe wrist. Not the primary gait actuator in MK.I.

### 3.8 Soft exosuit textile actuators
Wearable ribbons that pull along the limb instead of a rigid hinge. Wrong as the primary EcoSuit architecture — the design already committed to a rigid internal frame and ceramic segments — but useful under the plates as a second layer that unloads the shoulder or assists glove close without another gearbox.

